# Change: Add governed Codex Skill updates

## Why

Scheme C keeps the current OpenSpec, Superpowers, and Companion combination, but
its third-party dependency maintenance is still procedural. The repository can
describe how to compare upstream versions, validate a candidate, synchronize a
runtime, and roll back, yet nothing currently performs or schedules that audit.

The gap is visible in the current Superpowers installation: a Skill symlink
points at a locally patched Git checkout that is ahead of and behind upstream.
The symlink never fetches upstream, `codex update` does not update this checkout,
and a blind pull would risk losing the local patch or importing a Major workflow
change without approval.

The workflow therefore needs detection automation without mutation automation:
registered dependencies should be checked regularly and on demand, while every
actual update remains plan-bound, explicitly approved, validated, reviewable,
and reversible.

The first source-worktree attempt later exposed an approval-chain integrity
failure: its files matched a newer proposal revision, but the worktree retained
only an older Major manifest and lacked the newer proposal/tasks snapshots.
That attempt is quarantined as `BLOCKED_SOURCE_WORKTREE_RECOVERY`; it is not
repaired, reused, deleted, or treated as implementation evidence.

## What Changes

- Add an independently maintained Codex Skill named `codex-skill-update` in a
  new sibling source tree intended to become its own Git repository.
- Provide narrow natural-language and deterministic `$codex-skill-update`
  entrypoints for non-mutating `audit`, `plan`, and `verify`. State-changing
  apply, initial bootstrap, post-success rollback, registry replacement,
  schedule installation/removal/replacement, and cleanup return to Router-only
  internal transactions; do not patch the Codex CLI or claim a native
  `codex skill update` subcommand exists.
- Make public and scheduled observation/notification behavior a closed set of
  reviewed adapters with fixed executable/argv schemas. Registry values remain
  data and cannot inject a command, fetch, marketplace refresh, shell fragment,
  or notification program.
- Add a JSON managed-Skill registry and version ledger that keep tested
  compatibility baseline, effective local version, latest observed upstream
  version, and installation-channel available version separate. Bind the exact
  initial registry bytes/schema/revision, state layout, group closure, and
  adapter identities into bootstrap approval; any later registry replacement
  is a separately approved transaction and invalidates the prior schedule
  execution binding.
- Support explicit installation semantics for source-to-runtime copies,
  Git-checkout-plus-symlink installs, Codex marketplace plugins, and installer
  snapshots.
- Install a Monday 10:00 local one-shot read-only audit for the current macOS
  user through a separately planned, approved, backed-up, and verified schedule
  install/remove/replace transaction bound to the exact label, domain, plist,
  argv/runtime hashes, cadence, effective loaded configuration, controller state
  root, registry revision, complete dependency-group closure, closed-adapter
  executable/code/argv-schema identities, exact content-addressed controller
  payload/runtime lock and active discovery target, pre-state, and expiry. The
  LaunchAgent runs that exact payload rather than a movable discovery symlink.
  Job execution checks the complete binding before any package, network, or
  notification activity. It may write sanitized controller reports but cannot
  mutate managed Skills, live Git checkouts, plugin marketplaces, or
  installation snapshots and cannot chain into apply. It emits a minimal local
  notification only for update/blocker/audit-failure results.
- Generate immutable, expiring, hash-bound update plans. Require a
  Router-recorded action/target/scope/expiry-bound approval artifact for exact
  apply/bootstrap, post-success rollback, registry, schedule, or cleanup
  mutations. Automatic compensation after an approved mutation starts uses a
  durable single-use lease created while that approval is current; expiry or
  revocation stops forward progress but cannot authorize anything beyond exact
  pre-state restoration.
- Record revocation in a closed Router-owned append-only authority channel that
  does not wait for the transaction-long operation lock. A separate short
  authority lock linearizes revocation with each forward boundary from the
  final approval check through durable intent, atomic mutation, fsync, and phase
